# <%= current_page.data.title %>

This page exists so you can see a working page before writing your own, and so the
`docs-site` skill has a real section index to update. Delete it once you have
content of your own.

## Frontmatter

Every page needs the four keys at the top of this file:

| Key | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `title` | Shown in the sidebar, the browser tab, and as the `<h1>` via the ERB line below the frontmatter |
| `weight` | Sidebar ordering within the folder. Lower appears first. |
| `last_reviewed_on` | Drives the "last reviewed" notice and the review reminder |
| `review_in` | How long until the page is flagged as needing review, e.g. `6 months` |

Use gaps of 10 for `weight` (10, 20, 30…) so a page can be inserted later without
renumbering every sibling.

One convention worth keeping: a section's `index.html.md.erb` uses `weight: 1`, and
its pages start at `10`. The index's weight orders that *section* among its siblings
while page weights order pages *within* it — and because both live in the same folder,
letting them share a number makes the sidebar order unstable.

## Writing

Pages are Markdown with ERB, in files named `.html.md.erb`. Ordinary Markdown works —
headings, lists, tables, code blocks:

```bash
echo "fenced code blocks are syntax highlighted"
```

Link to another page in the same folder with its built `.html` name, not the source
filename: `[Example Page](example-page.html)`.

Link to a page in another folder relatively: `[Home](../index.html)`.

## Images

Put images in `source/assets/images/` and reference them with a root-relative path:

```markdown
![Description of what the image shows](/assets/images/my-diagram.png)
```

Always write real alt text. It is what a screen reader announces, and the link
checker is configured to allow missing alt text rather than enforce it — so nothing
will catch an empty one for you.

## Previewing

```bash
bundle exec middleman server   # http://localhost:4567
```

The site rebuilds as you save.
